---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const project = {
  title: "Making Arithmetic Transformers Generalizable and Interpretable",
  tag: "ML Research",
};
---

<Layout title={project.title}>
  <a href={base} class="back">← Back to projects</a>
  <article>
    <span class="tag">{project.tag}</span>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>

    <p class="summary">
      A 2M-parameter Transformer, trained only on numbers up to 10 digits long, generalizes
      to 200-digit addition with no measurable accuracy drop. This project identifies the
      design choices (positional encodings, output representations, and intermediate
      computation) that determine whether a model truly learns an algorithm or merely
      memorizes training patterns.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">
      ETH Zürich · Deep Learning 2025 · with Zhaorui Gong, Jakub Kaššák, and Yiqu Yang
    </p>

    <div class="actions">
      <a href={`${base}papers/arithmetic-transformers.pdf`} class="btn" download>Download Paper (PDF)</a>
      <a href={`${base}slides/arithmetic-transformers.pdf`} class="btn btn-outline" target="_blank">View Presentation</a>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why this matters</h2>
      <p>
        Length generalization, the ability to solve problems larger than any seen during
        training, is one of the core unsolved challenges in building reliable AI systems.
        It matters far beyond arithmetic: the same failure mode appears whenever a model needs
        to reason over long contexts, compose skills, or handle edge cases outside its training
        distribution. Addition is a clean testbed because the correct algorithm is known,
        which makes it possible to measure not just whether a model succeeds, but
        <em> why</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Models trained on 10-digit addition routinely achieve near-perfect training accuracy but
        fail completely on 11-digit inputs. We set out to understand what separates models that
        generalize from models that don't. We found that architectural choices that look
        cosmetic on the surface have dramatic effects on extrapolation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The core finding: structure in, generalization out</h2>
      <p>
        The most important variable is how the model represents position. Standard positional
        encodings give each token a single index, treating the problem as a flat sequence.
        We found that giving the model a two-dimensional position (one axis for which digit
        place a token occupies, another for which number it belongs to) produces dramatically
        better generalization. This forces the model to learn an operation over aligned digit
        columns rather than a pattern over raw sequence positions, which is much closer to how
        addition actually works.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second key variable is how carry information is represented in the output. When the
        model must infer carry state implicitly from surrounding digits, it struggles to
        generalize. Augmenting the output vocabulary with explicit carry tokens, a small
        signal that marks which output digits triggered a carry, removes this inference
        burden and consistently improves both accuracy and extrapolation range.
      </p>
      <p>
        The best configuration pairs two-dimensional positional encoding with explicit carry
        tokens in a 2-layer, 2-head model. Trained on 10-digit numbers, it achieves over 90%
        exact sequence accuracy on 200-digit numbers, a 20× extrapolation. Notably, smaller
        architectures outperform larger ones once the right inductive biases are in place,
        which suggests that model scale is not the bottleneck; representation is.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Scaling to many operands</h2>
      <p>
        Adding many numbers together is harder than adding two, because the model must
        generalize across both digit length and operand count simultaneously. We tackled
        this by introducing a scratchpad: instead of jumping straight to the final answer,
        the model generates running cumulative sums as intermediate steps, much like showing
        your work. This breaks the problem into a chain of two-operand additions, each
        building on the last.
      </p>
      <p>
        We extended the two-dimensional positional scheme to handle this structure, adding a
        third axis to distinguish input tokens from scratchpad tokens. A subtle but consequential
        implementation detail: the separator tokens between scratchpad steps must be associated
        with the following partial sum, not the preceding one, because the model predicts the
        next digit immediately after each separator. Getting this wrong eliminated generalization
        entirely.
      </p>
      <p>
        Trained on problems with up to 10 operands and 10 digits each, our 15.5M-parameter
        model achieves 79% exact sequence accuracy on problems with 40 operands of 40 digits,
        requiring it to predict 1,849 consecutive tokens correctly. It also maintains perfect
        accuracy on all tasks smaller than its training range, demonstrating clean
        bidirectional generalization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Opening the black box</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond achieving strong performance, we wanted to understand <em>how</em> the model
        computes. We analyzed attention patterns and used the tuned lens, a probing technique
        that estimates what each intermediate layer is predicting, to trace how arithmetic
        is decomposed across the network.
      </p>
      <p>
        The attention maps told a clear story: the first layer focuses on aligning each output
        digit with the corresponding input digits from the same column, exactly the right
        information for digit-wise addition. The tuned lens confirmed that the first layer alone
        already achieves near-perfect accuracy; the model essentially solves the problem in one
        pass and uses the second layer only to clean up carry propagation.
      </p>
      <p>
        To test this interpretation, we trained a variant without explicit carry tokens. In that
        model, the second layer's attention shifted dramatically toward less-significant positions,
        and first-layer accuracy collapsed to roughly 4%. The model was forced to look backwards
        to reconstruct carry information it could no longer read directly, pushing the computation
        into deeper layers. This is a concrete, mechanistic explanation for why explicit carry
        tokens matter, and a demonstration that interpretability tools can reveal not just
        what a model does, but why design choices work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I took away</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest lesson from this project is that inductive bias, building the right structure
        into how the model sees its inputs and outputs, matters more than raw capacity.
        A well-designed small model consistently beat larger models with naive representations.
        We also found that curriculum learning, which is often assumed to help on structured
        tasks, reliably hurt generalization here: models trained on progressively harder examples
        developed a pathological carry reflex that broke their performance on simple inputs.
        The right training distribution, not the right training schedule, was the decisive variable.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the interpretability side, this project reinforced that attention patterns and probing
        techniques can yield genuine mechanistic insight and not just post-hoc rationalization,
        when combined with controlled ablations.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-decoration: none; color: #555; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .back:hover { color: #1a1a1a; }
  .tag { font-size: 0.72rem; background: #f0f4ff; color: #3b5bdb; padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem; border-radius: 999px; display: inline-block; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 650; margin-top: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; color: #1a1a1a; }
  .summary { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: #333; max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .meta { font-size: 0.88rem; color: #888; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  section { max-width: 680px; }
  p { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #333; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .actions { display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 0.65rem 1.25rem; background: #3b5bdb; color: #fff; border-radius: 7px; text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 500; }
  .btn:hover { background: #2f4ac7; }
  .btn-outline { background: #fff; color: #3b5bdb; border: 1.5px solid #3b5bdb; }
  .btn-outline:hover { background: #f0f4ff; }
</style>
